A||||11||mrfixit58||Fiero Radiator Fan and V-8 Motors - Archie or Other SBC Owners??||||5||
Z||000000||mrfixit58||07-10-2001||04:28 PM||||This question is only partially related to the Fiero but with all the V-8 conversions folk on this site it seem appropriate to ask the question here.<P>I'm installing a 89 TPI motor into a 68 Camaro. The MAF looks like it will prohibit me using a mechanical fan. I pulled a fan and shroud from one of my Fiero parts cars and held it against the Camaro radiator and it's a pretty good fit. It covers totally from top to bottom but lacks about one inch on each side from covering 100%. <P>My questions is: Is the fan going to pull enough air through the radiator to keep the motor cool? I am using the 700r4 transmission and am going to run the Vintage Air A/C system. Again, do you think it'll be a problem. Lastly, I read in a resent car magazine (either Car Craft or Super Chevy) that the Pontiac transport uses a 240 watt fan motor. Does anyone know what the wattage of the Fiero's fan motor? If it's smaller an upgrade may help.<P>Thanks,<P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Hartz||07-10-2001||05:02 PM||||One thing to consider might be upgrading the motor to a Speed Density system.  My Fiero has a 1989 TPI motor converted to Speed Density, and that allowed me to put an open element K&N cone on the front of it.  Might give you more options.<P>Just a thought,<BR>Hartz<BR>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo||||reg||1
Z||000002||Archie||07-10-2001||07:25 PM||||I'll assume that you want to stick with MAF because you already have the correct ECM and wire harness for it &/or you have a non-stock cam and know that MAF compensates somewhat better for non-stock cams.<P>You could use a 90* air tube to relocate the MAF, like this one.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mvc-630s_1.jpg"> <P>That unit is GM p/n 25147187||||reg||1
Z||000003||Will||07-10-2001||07:28 PM||||I wouldn't call speed-density and "upgrade" over a MAF based system, but whatever works.<P>Use the electric fan and see if you can find a heavy duty fan motor. The Fiero already has the large 16" fan. The shroud will need modifying to hold the 4" motor housing, though.<BR>Look at fans and go by the picture they publish in that magazine (the one you want has two vent holes which may or may not be plugged). Not all 4" fan motors are the heavy duty ones.<P>We just went through a heavy duty fan motor search last weekend and found out a lot of this stuff.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Joe Torma||07-10-2001||08:28 PM||||Hi,<P>I got a 16" fan from JC whitney and used the origial shroud from the fiero.  Delivers 1949cfm.  It's only 3 3/8 deep--perfect to shove air out of a hood vent.  I discovered that if you cut the 4 posts off the motor @ the shroud, you can silicone/screw the new fan inside the shroud and it looks stock!  I kept the shroud for effeciency and it was even easier to install!<P>Joe<BR>3400 series 300hp<p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 07-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfixit58||07-10-2001||09:04 PM||||Thanks guys,<P>I plan to keep the MAF because, basically, I'm cheap.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> I'm not only using the motor and tranny from the 89 Firebird, I'm also using the ENTIRE wiring harness -- from headlights to taillights. I've already mocked the engine up and it runs and the accessories work.<P>The fan is the only real major concern right now. I would still like to know the Fiero fan motor wattage.<P>Will -- You do -- off hand -- remember which model of cars you saw the heavy duty fan motor?<P>Archie -- the tube you show is very similar to the one I'm using. I'm also using the donor A/C, brackets,  and other accessories so I have to get the tube out past these devices. I can't go above them because of the hood. I'm relocating the battery to the trunk and mounting the new air filter canister in the old battery location. About the fan, do you just use the stock Fiero units on your conversions or what?<P>Joe -- Do you remember the cost of the fan from JCW? If the larger motor and stock Fiero fan doesn't do the job, I may consider your idea.<P>Thanks,<BR><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||14
Z||000006||Joe Torma||07-10-2001||09:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrfixit58:<BR><B>Thanks guys,<P>Joe -- Do you remember the cost of the fan from JCW? If the larger motor and stock Fiero fan doesn't do the job, I may consider your idea.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If you need the adjustable themostat(I would recommend it!)model, I got mine for like $90.00(find a sale catalog) of you don't, and use the stock one(well you probably won't since you have a V8) you can just get the fan for less.<P>I don't think the wattage is what you should be concerned about...it is the cfm.  My stock fan always kicked on and the voltmeter would twitch quite a bit.  This fan has more blades and is super smooth(I think the stock fan likes to vibrate more than pull air). The Whitney fan draws 11 amps.<P>Joe<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||mrfixit58||07-10-2001||11:07 PM||||Joe,<P>If your fan is only drawing 11 amps, it's only around 140 watts (11 amps x 12 volts x ? powerfactor). The larger the wattage the better the motor will turn the fan without bogging.   The higher wattage motors can take advantage of their power by using a fan blade with more pitch. More pitch and more fan speed equals more air. Awesome combination if you can find it.<P><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||3
Z||000008||bad911||07-11-2001||01:36 AM||||I'm doing a 4.9 swap and you lit my curiosity. Checked my Caddy boneyard-booty and both these puppies pulled 15 amps @ 13.3v hooked up to m'lady's running truck. The left one is '86 Deville 20w x 17.5h. Right is '91 Seville and is 17h x 17.5w. Both are 5" deep with the shroud and 4.5 just motor and fan. Fans are 16". Hope this helps.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Cad_fans.jpg">  <P><p>[This message has been edited by bad911 (edited 07-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||mrfixit58||07-11-2001||07:58 AM||||bad911,<P>The shroud you show off of the 86 is the same size as the Fiero's. The only difference is the Fiero fan is centered. The motors are close to 200 watts. That's pretty good. I'll keep it in mind if I can't find the elusive 240 watt motor.<P>Thanks,<BR><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||14
Z||000010||Cozmo||07-11-2001||08:36 AM||||Roy, I know you are "cheap"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> but there are some listed in the new Summit catalog that may work.  I agree with Joe, CFM is the more critical measurement.  Proper shrouding is important too, as you got to "move the air" in the right places.<BR>   The Summit fans run $100-$200 and have outputs up to 2400 CFM.  You might want to get some measurements of the size you need and go visit the local scrapyard, you might find something that would fit and work fine...<BR>BTW, I've heard of people using dual VW Sirocco fans on some Street Rod applications.<BR>Can't wait to see the car!<BR><B>C02M0</B>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Joe Torma||07-11-2001||09:47 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cozmo:<BR><B>Roy, I know you are "cheap"   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> but there are some listed in the new Summit catalog that may work.  I agree with Joe, CFM is the more critical measurement<P>The Summit fans run $100-$200 and have outputs up to 2400 CFM.  [b]C02M0</B>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hmmm...didn't see the 2400cfm one.  Probably should have gotten that one.  Oh well...but if you need the fan constantly when driving you should get a 4 core radiator anyway.<BR>Last one I called about was like $250.<P>Joe<BR>||||reg||1
